The ATLAS experiment at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC) has achieved precise measurements in two areas, namely neutral B meson mixing and CP violation, as well as in rare electroweak processes occurring in suppressed B-meson decays. This study concentrates on recent findings from the ATLAS collaboration, encompassing evaluations of rare occurrences like $B^0_s \to \mu^+\mu^-$ and $B^0 \to \mu^+\mu^-$ decays and the determination of CP-violating processes in $B^0_s \to J/\psi\phi$. Additionally, it provides an overview of the effective lifetime of the $B^0_s$ meson. A summary of current ATLAS and CMS findings is presented, incorporating results from 36 inverse femtobarns of data collected by ATLAS at 13 TeV and 40 inverse femtobarns of data collected by CMS at 8 and 13 TeV. A theoretical prediction for the total branching fraction of the $B^0_s \to J/\psi\phi$ decay, considering radiative corrections, is provided, leading to a projected sensitivity for a 36 inverse femtobarns 13 TeV data set. A review of experimental techniques and analyses used by ATLAS is also included, along with an evaluation of rare decay processes' sensitivity to electroweak precision parameters and the matter-antimatter asymmetry problem. The study concludes with an evaluation of the current discrepancy between measured and predicted branching fractions for the $B^0 \to K^+K^-$ decay. 

