This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. This investigation is an extension of previous work by R. Aouaoui and S. Kella in [Nonlinear Analysis: Real World Applications 48 (2020)] for $\mu=0$ and $\Delta$-Laplacian. Notably, this research introduces new techniques to address $\Delta^2$-equations and provides a foundation for exploring similar problems. Additionally, we verify the optimal regularity of the normalized ground state solution within the space $H^2(\mathbb{R}^4)$. Furthermore, we demonstrate the sensitivity of the normalized ground state solution with respect to the nonlocal nonlinearity, revealing a symmetry breaking phenomenon. Lastly, we estimate the convergence of a sequence of solutions under restricted conditions. This study is accompanied by an animation illustrating the symmetry breaking of the normalized ground state solution. As the parameter $\beta$ decreases from $9$ to $0$, the symmetry of the solution is broken continuously. Animation can be found at the reference [20].</system>

